",\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1145/3297858.3304023\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"Oper. Syst. pp. 1001\\u20131014 . 50 A. Kandala et al. \\u201cHardware-efficient variational quantum eigensolver\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2019\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.5555/1973124\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"tion Cambridge MA USA: MIT Press . tance on quantum addition circuits \\u201d J. Emerg. Technol. Comput. Syst\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2011\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": \"1725.20117\",\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.5555/2011725.2011732\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"12 L. Gyongyosi and S. Imre \\u201cRouting space exploration for scalable routing pp. 383\\u2013391\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2007\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": \"1827.20118\",\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.5555/2011827.2011828\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"11 L. Gyongyosi and S. Imre \\u201cEntanglement concentration service for Comput. vol. 4 pp. 237\\u2013251\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2004\n        }\n      ],\n      \"related_link_enrichment\": {\n        \"skip_reason\": null,\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"references\"\n        ],\n        \"status\": \"enriched\",\n        \"warnings\": [\n          \"Skipped 2 unsupported external-link candidate(s).\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"schema_version\": \"source_record_v1\",\n      \"source_date_inference\": {\n        \"confidence\": \"high\",\n        \"date\": \"2021-01-22\",\n        \"origin\": \"source_markdown_header\",\n        \"rationale\": \"The extracted front matter states that the article was received August 3, 2020, revised October 19, 2020, accepted January 19, 2021, and published January 22, 2021.\"\n      },\n      \"source_dossier\": {\n        \"sections\": [\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The paper treats distributed quantum computing as a way to combine remote QPUs into a larger virtual processor, but compilation must translate a logical circuit into operations that respect both local coupling maps and network resources. This turns remote CNOT placement, scheduling, and Bell-state availability into compiler-level concerns rather than merely hardware details.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"0\",\n              \"1\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Distributed Compilation Problem\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"Remote CNOTs depend on Bell states stored in communication qubits. Because each remote CNOT consumes a Bell state and because simultaneous remote gates are limited by communication-qubit availability, distributed compilers must optimize around a slower and more resource-constrained class of operations than local gates.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"2\",\n              \"3\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Remote Operation Constraints\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"Entanglement swapping gives a distributed processor virtual links beyond physical nearest-neighbor quantum links, which increases the set of possible remote interactions. The same mechanism consumes Bell states at intermediate processors, so added connectivity is not free and must be weighed against EPR-pair generation and scheduling cost.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"4\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Connectivity Versus EPR Cost\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The analysis intentionally chooses a restrictive architecture: one data qubit per QPU and a one-dimensional nearest-neighbor network. This makes every original CNOT remote and maximizes path lengths, allowing the resulting bounds to be read as conservative upper limits rather than average-case predictions for richer topologies.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"5\",\n              \"6\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Worst-Case Analytical Model\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The entanglement-swapping strategy directly realizes remote CNOTs after preparing virtual links and has a linear n/2-style bound per layer. The data-qubit-swapping strategy instead moves data qubits so interacting qubits become neighbors, yielding the paper's n/4-style theorem and explaining why repeated interaction patterns can be exploited.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"6\",\n              \"7\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Two Distribution Strategies\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The compiler implementation processes QASM circuits left to right, builds front layers of parallel CNOTs, and then compiles each front layer. Its O(dn2) distribution complexity is central to the claimed efficiency because the approach is intended to handle medium-to-large circuits where optimal distributed compilation is impractical.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"8\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Implementation And Complexity\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The performance section validates the theoretical upper bounds and reports that data-qubit swapping generally improves link-generation layers and circuit depth relative to entanglement swapping alone. The comparisons with prior work must be interpreted cautiously because the competing compiler benefits from a more favorable hypercube topology.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"9\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Experimental Interpretation\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"summary_long\": \"The source addresses a specific bottleneck in distributed quantum computing: a quantum algorithm usually starts as a hardware-agnostic circuit, but a distributed machine imposes both local processor constraints and network-mediated constraints on remote two-qubit gates. Ferrari, Cacciapuoti, Amoretti, and Caleffi frame distributed QPUs as a virtual quantum processor enabled by the Quantum Internet, but emphasize that remote operations are expensive because they consume Bell states, require communication qubits, and depend on classical signaling and entanglement regeneration. The paper's analytical contribution is deliberately conservative. It models a worst-case distributed architecture with one data qubit per QPU and a one-dimensional nearest-neighbor topology, ensuring that every CNOT in the original circuit becomes remote and that nonadjacent communication is maximally constrained. Under this model, the entanglement-swapping strategy has a per-layer depth bound proportional to n/2, with hardware constants for link entanglement, Bell-state measurement, and remote CNOT execution. A second strategy swaps data qubits through the network so that the CNOTs in a layer eventually act on neighboring processors; Theorem 1 bounds its per-layer depth by n/4 dqs plus a constant term. The implementation reads QASM circuits left to right, builds front layers of parallel CNOTs, and compiles those layers using either entanglement swapping or data-qubit swapping. Its overall distribution complexity is O(dn2), so the design is intended to be general-purpose, computationally efficient, and effective in the sense that worst-case depth overhead grows linearly with circuit width. The experiments validate the derived bounds on medium-size circuits and compare both strategies with Andr\\u00e9s-Mart\\u00ednez and Heunen's compiler, noting that the comparison is structurally unfavorable because the other compiler assumes a hypercube topology. The practical takeaway is not that one strategy always dominates under every architecture, but that data-qubit movement can reduce link-generation layers and compiled depth when CNOT interaction patterns repeat, while topology, available communication qubits, and EPR consumption remain decisive design variables.\",\n        \"summary_short\": \"This IEEE Transactions on Quantum Engineering article studies how to compile quantum circuits for distributed quantum computers connected through quantum-network resources. It identifies remote-operation constraints, derives conservative depth-overhead bounds for two CNOT distribution strategies, implements the compiler in Qiskit, and validates the analysis on benchmark circuits.\"\n      },\n      \"source_extraction\": {\n        \"converter_name\": \"pdftotext\",\n        \"converter_version\": null,\n        \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n        \"status\": \"success\",\n        \"warnings\": []\n      },\n      \"source_id\": \"source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d\",\n      \"source_kind\": \"file\",\n      \"source_locator\": \"03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing.pdf\",\n      \"source_media_type\": \"application/pdf\",\n      \"source_semantic\": {\n        \"authors\": [\n          \"Davide Ferrari\",\n          \"Angela Sara Cacciapuoti\",\n          \"Michele Amoretti\",\n          \"Marcello Caleffi\"\n        ],\n        \"display_title\": \"Compiler Design for Distributed Quantum Computing\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1109/TQE.2021.3053921\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d\",\n        \"venue\": \"IEEE Transactions on Quantum Engineering\"\n      },\n      \"source_type\": \"file\",\n      \"summary\": \"Ferrari, Cacciapuoti, Amoretti, and Caleffi analyze compiler design for distributed quantum computing, derive linear worst-case overhead bounds for remote-CNOT compilation strategies, implement a Qiskit-based compiler, and validate the bounds and strategy tradeoffs on medium-size quantum circuits.\",\n      \"title\": \"03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing\",\n      \"warnings\": [\n        \"The source record date was null, but the source markdown front matter names January 22, 2021 as the date of publication.\",\n        \"The markdown extraction is marked usable, but Table 1 numeric cells are not reliably preserved in the extracted text; evidence from the table is therefore represented qualitatively.\",\n        \"Existing claim and relation files in the provided context refer to earlier compiler sources, so generated relations reference the newly extracted claims by index rather than existing canonical claim IDs.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"analysis_policy\": {\n        \"fallback_artifacts\": [\n          \"source_file\"\n        ],\n        \"preferred_artifact\": \"source_markdown\",\n        \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n        \"warnings\": []\n      },\n      \"article_kind\": null,\n      \"artifact_root\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e\",\n      \"artifacts\": {\n        \"front_page_image\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e/front_page.png\",\n        \"source_extraction\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e/source_extraction.json\",\n        \"source_file\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e/source.pdf\",\n        \"source_markdown\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e/source.md\",\n        \"source_provenance\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e/source_provenance.md\"\n      },\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Heiko Falk\",\n        \"Paul Lokuciejewski\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_count\": null,\n      \"citation_count_as_of\": null,\n      \"citation_count_confidence\": \"unknown\",\n      \"citation_count_provider\": \"unknown\",\n      \"date\": null,\n      \"display_title\": \"WCC Compiler Reduces Worst-Case Execution Times\",\n      \"external_related_links\": [\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"doi.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"canonical_paper\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_doi\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[3].doi\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Derived from a DOI found in the source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"DOI\",\n          \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11241-010-9101-x\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ingested_at\": \"2026-04-26T17:50:43Z\",\n      \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n      \"references\": [\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"WCET-aware Compilation\",\n          \"url\": \"http://ls12-www.cs.tu-dortmund.de/research/activities/wcc\",\n          \"year\": 2010\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"AbsInt Angewandte Informatik GmbH aiT: worst-case execution time analyzers\",\n          \"url\": \"http://www\",\n          \"year\": 2010\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"Informatik Centrum Dortmund e. V. 2010b ICD-C compiler framework\",\n          \"url\": \"http://www.icd.de/es/icd-c\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1007/s11241-010-9101-x\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"DOI\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": null\n        }\n      ],\n      \"related_link_enrichment\": {\n        \"skip_reason\": null,\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"references\"\n        ],\n        \"status\": \"enriched\",\n        \"warnings\": [\n          \"Skipped 3 unsupported external-link candidate(s).\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"schema_version\": \"source_record_v1\",\n      \"source_date_inference\": {\n        \"confidence\": \"high\",\n        \"date\": \"2010-07-22\",\n        \"origin\": \"source.md front matter\",\n        \"rationale\": \"The extracted markdown states \\u201cPublished online: 22 July 2010\\u201d immediately below the authors.\"\n      },\n      \"source_dossier\": {\n        \"sections\": [\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The paper\\u2019s motivating claim is that hard real-time development cannot rely on average-case compiler optimization or late manual timing checks. WCET reduction is path-sensitive: optimizing a block or a noncritical path may not reduce the global bound. This makes the compiler\\u2019s lack of timing knowledge a root cause rather than a minor tooling gap.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"0\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Problem and Motivation\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"WCC addresses the tooling gap by embedding aiT into compilation. The compiler converts its low-level representation into CRL2, invokes aiT without making the user manage analyzer setup, and imports value, loop-bound, cache, pipeline, and path-analysis results. This turns WCET analysis from a separate post-build activity into an optimization input.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"1\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Compiler-Analyzer Integration\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"A central architectural move is back-annotation from assembly-level structures to source-level ICD-C structures. The paper treats this mapping carefully because basic blocks do not always correspond one-to-one across IRs. Once mapped, WCC can expose WCEP membership, edge execution frequencies, cache misses, code size, and spill-code data to high-level transformations.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"2\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Back-Annotation as Bridge\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"Static WCET analysis needs safe loop iteration bounds, and manually supplying them is tedious and error-prone. WCC\\u2019s loop analyzer substantially automates this part: the reported benchmark table covers 707 loops with 99% analyzability and 96% exactness. Slicing and polyhedral evaluation then address analysis cost, with major runtime reductions on examples such as fft and matmul.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"3\",\n              \"4\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Flow Facts and Loop Bounds\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"Procedure cloning and positioning show why WCET information is useful above assembly level. Cloning specializes functions so data-dependent loop bounds become constant in important call contexts, improving WCET precision but increasing code size. Positioning reorders functions using WCET-derived call frequencies to improve instruction-cache behavior without relying on potentially unrepresentative profiling inputs.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"5\",\n              \"6\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Source-Level Optimizations\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The scratchpad-memory results show the value of assembly-level optimization guided by WCET data. The code allocator chooses beneficial basic-block regions for fast program SPM, while the data allocator chooses frequently accessed variables for fast data SPM. Reported average reductions are substantial, and the code-size or solver overhead is described as negligible in the evaluated setting.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"7\",\n              \"8\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Memory Allocation Optimizations\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The register allocator deliberately protects the WCEP from spill code, even if that can increase total text size. Across 46 benchmarks it reduces WCET by 31.2% on average versus traditional graph coloring. The matmult sequence then demonstrates practical composition: register allocation, scratchpad allocation, and procedure positioning together cut the reported WCET by more than 70%.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"9\",\n              \"10\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Register Allocation and Composition\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The paper\\u2019s strongest claims are grounded in a specific toolchain and target: WCC, aiT, and Infineon TriCore processors. The results therefore support the feasibility and effectiveness of this integrated architecture, but they should not be read as architecture-independent speedups. Several optimizations also involve trade-offs, especially analysis time and code-size growth.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"1\",\n              \"5\",\n              \"7\",\n              \"8\",\n              \"9\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Assumptions and Limits\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"summary_long\": \"Falk and Lokuciejewski frame WCC as a response to a practical real-time-systems problem: ordinary compilers optimize primarily for average-case behavior, while industrial real-time workflows often disable optimizations and perform WCET analysis only after code generation. The paper argues that this separation is both costly and technically weak, because WCET reduction depends on knowing the worst-case execution path and on understanding how local changes affect global timing. WCC therefore integrates the aiT static WCET analyzer into the compiler, translates low-level compiler IR into aiT\\u2019s CRL2 format, invokes aiT transparently, imports timing results, and back-annotates those results to source-level ICD-C structures. This infrastructure supports automated loop-bound analysis, source-level optimizations such as procedure cloning and positioning, and assembly-level optimizations such as scratchpad allocation and WCET-aware register allocation. The evidence base is largely benchmark-driven. The loop analyzer handles 707 loops across 96 benchmarks with 99% analyzability and 96% exactness, while slicing and polyhedral evaluation reduce analysis time sharply on selected examples. Procedure cloning can reduce WCET estimates dramatically when call contexts make loop bounds constant, though the paper is candid about code-size expansion. Procedure positioning improves cache behavior using WCET-derived call frequencies. ILP-based code and data scratchpad allocation reduce WCET by prioritizing regions and variables with high worst-case benefit. Register allocation avoids spill code on the WCEP and gives the largest average reduction among the reported individual optimizations. The final matmult experiment shows that several optimizations can compose, reducing WCET from 775,720 cycles to 227,794 cycles. The main limitation is that the results are tied to the WCC/TriCore/aiT environment and to benchmark suites, but the paper provides a coherent architecture and quantitative evidence for WCET-aware compilation as a system-level approach.\",\n        \"summary_short\": \"This source presents WCC, a C compiler framework designed to optimize hard real-time software for worst-case execution time rather than average-case speed. Its central idea is to integrate static WCET analysis into compilation, lift timing information across compiler abstraction levels, and use that information to drive source-level and assembly-level optimizations.\"\n      },\n      \"source_extraction\": {\n        \"converter_name\": \"pdftotext\",\n        \"converter_version\": null,\n        \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n        \"status\": \"success\",\n        \"warnings\": []\n      },\n      \"source_id\": \"source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e\",\n      \"source_kind\": \"file\",\n      \"source_locator\": \"04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times.pdf\",\n      \"source_media_type\": \"application/pdf\",\n      \"source_semantic\": {\n        \"authors\": [\n          \"Heiko Falk\",\n          \"Paul Lokuciejewski\"\n        ],\n        \"display_title\": \"WCC Compiler Reduces Worst-Case Execution Times\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1007/s11241-010-9101-x\",\n        \"publication_venue\": \"Real-Time Systems\",\n        \"record_display_title\": \"Untitled Source\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e\"\n      },\n      \"source_type\": \"file\",\n      \"summary\": \"Falk and Lokuciejewski describe WCC, a WCET-aware C compiler that integrates static timing analysis into compilation and uses the resulting WCEP, loop-bound, cache, scratchpad, and register-allocation information to reduce worst-case execution times. The paper combines infrastructure claims with benchmark evidence for loop analysis, procedure cloning and positioning, scratchpad allocation, register allocation, and a composed matmult case study.\",\n      \"title\": \"04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times\",\n      \"warnings\": [\n        {\n          \"code\": \"source_record_title_was_placeholder\",\n          \"message\": \"The task_context source record display title was \\u201cUntitled Source\\u201d; title, authors, DOI, and publication date were recovered from source.md.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"code\": \"markdown_extraction_used\",\n          \"message\": \"source_extraction.json reported usable markdown with no warnings, so the binary PDF was not opened for layout recovery.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"analysis_policy\": {\n        \"fallback_artifacts\": [\n          \"source_file\"\n        ],\n        \"preferred_artifact\": \"source_markdown\",\n        \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n        \"warnings\": []\n      },\n      \"article_kind\": null,\n      \"artifact_root\": \"sources/artifacts/source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69\",\n      \"artifacts\": {\n        \"front_page_image\": \"sources/artifacts/source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69/front_page.png\",\n        \"source_extraction\": \"sources/artifacts/source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69/source_extraction.json\",\n        \"source_file\": \"sources/artifacts/source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69/source.pdf\",\n        \"source_markdown\": \"sources/artifacts/source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69/source.md\",\n        \"source_provenance\": \"sources/artifacts/source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69/source_provenance.md\"\n      },\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Anupama J. Thubagere\",\n        \"Chris Thachuk\",\n        \"Joseph Berleant\",\n        \"Robert F. Johnson\",\n        \"Diana A. Ardelean\",\n        \"Kevin M. Cherry\",\n        \"Lulu Qian\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_count\": null,\n      \"citation_count_as_of\": null,\n      \"citation_count_confidence\": \"unknown\",\n      \"citation_count_provider\": \"unknown\",\n      \"date\": null,\n      \"display_title\": \"Compiler-Aided DNA Strand Displacement Circuits\",\n      \"external_related_links\": [\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"doi.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"canonical_paper\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_doi\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[11].doi\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Derived from a DOI found in the source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"Received 7 Aug | Accepted 21 Dec | Published 23 Feb 2017 OPEN\",\n          \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms14373\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"arxiv.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"research_index\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_url\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[9].url\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Captured from normalized source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/\",\n          \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.0782\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"arxiv.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"research_index\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_url\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[6].url\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Captured from normalized source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"pseudoknotted secondary structures. Preprint at /\",\n          \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"arxiv.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"research_index\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_arxiv\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[10].arxiv\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Derived from an arXiv identifier found in the source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"Referenced arXiv entry\",\n          \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.03738\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ingested_at\": \"2026-04-26T17:58:08Z\",\n      \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n      \"references\": [\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"26. Lakin M. et al.Visual DSD https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/project/ To view a copy of this license visit /\",\n          \"url\": \"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"19. Chandrasekaran A. R. Anderson N. Kizer M. Halvorsen K. & Wang X. Reprints and permission information is available online at /\",\n          \"url\": \"http://npg.nature.com\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"improve the building blocks such that they are substantially less \\ufb01les are available to download at the Seesaw Compiler website\",\n          \"url\": \"http://qianlab.cal-\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"27. Soloveichik D. CRNSimulator /\",\n          \"url\": \"http://users.ece.utexas.edu/~soloveichik\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"5636\\u20135646 . Supplementary Information accompanies this paper at /\",\n          \"url\": \"http://www.nature.com\",\n          \"year\": 2011\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"24. Qian L. Seesaw Compiler / International License. The images or other third party material in this\",\n          \"url\": \"http://www.qianlab.caltech.edu/SeesawCompiler\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"pseudoknotted secondary structures. Preprint at /\",\n          \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"kinetics of multiple interacting nucleic acid strands. LNCS 9211 194\\u2013211 Oligonucleotides /\",\n          \"url\": \"https://www.idtdna.com/pages/docs/technical-reports\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"26. Lakin M. et al.Visual DSD / To view a copy of this license visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\",\n          \"url\": \"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/project\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": \"1411.0782\",\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/\",\n          \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.0782\",\n          \"year\": 2014\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": \"1505.03738\",\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": null,\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2015\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1038/ncomms14373\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"Received 7 Aug | Accepted 21 Dec | Published 23 Feb 2017 OPEN\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2016\n        }\n      ],\n      \"related_link_enrichment\": {\n        \"skip_reason\": null,\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"references\"\n        ],\n        \"status\": \"enriched\",\n        \"warnings\": [\n          \"Skipped 8 unsupported external-link candidate(s).\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"schema_version\": \"source_record_v1\",\n      \"source_date_inference\": {\n        \"confidence\": \"high\",\n        \"date\": \"2017-02-23\",\n        \"origin\": \"source_markdown_front_matter\",\n        \"rationale\": \"The source markdown front matter states \\u201cPublished 23 Feb 2017\\u201d and gives DOI 10.1038/ncomms14373.\"\n      },\n      \"source_dossier\": {\n        \"sections\": [\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The paper grounds its software contribution in a concrete translation path: feedforward digital logic is converted to dual-rail logic, then to a seesaw DNA circuit, and then to Visual DSD, Mathematica, and sequence files. That makes the compiler a practical construction aid rather than only a high-level design notation. The workflow is especially important because the later experimental protocol depends on knowing which gates, thresholds, fuels, reporters, and signals should be assembled.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"claim-seesaw-compiler-automates-dna-circuit-design--367a566abb69\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Compiler-to-Molecule Workflow\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The benchmark circuit computes a combined rule 110 and rule 124 transition function, giving the experiment a nontrivial logic target. Its compiled seesaw implementation has six layers and 78 distinct initial DNA species. Those counts make the demonstration a test of composition, not just an isolated reaction, and explain why automated design and systematic debugging matter for this source.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"claim-rule-110-124-circuit-has-78-species--367a566abb69\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Scale of Demonstration\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The article frames accessibility as a core bottleneck for molecular programming. Unpurified standard-desalting oligonucleotides reduce experimental burden, but they introduce synthesis errors and effective-concentration uncertainty. The source\\u2019s main practical claim is therefore conditional: unpurified strands are usable for complex circuits when their nonideal behavior is measured, modeled, and corrected rather than ignored.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"claim-unpurified-strands-can-build-complex-circuits--367a566abb69\",\n              \"claim-signal-restoration-compensates-for-impurity--367a566abb69\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Why Unpurified Components Matter\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The strongest experimental reasoning comes from calibration values and outlier measurements. The authors use an estimated b/a ratio of 1.4 to choose nominal thresholds for OR and AND gates, then test gate behavior. When a composed two-layer circuit fails symmetrically, the gate-calibration data reveal unequal effective gate concentrations, including a gate near 0.44x. This evidence turns impurity from an opaque failure into a diagnosable parameter.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"claim-effective-concentration-calibration-guides-thresholds--367a566abb69\",\n              \"claim-outlier-gates-expose-synthesis-variation--367a566abb69\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Calibration and Outlier Handling\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The proposed workflow sequences calibration, outlier adjustment, and final output tuning. That structure is practically important because it gives novice or non-specialist users an ordered way to recover desired behavior without purifying every component. The reported three-day construction of the rule 110 sub-circuit is evidence for the procedure\\u2019s utility, but it should be read as a demonstrated case rather than a universal build-time bound.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"claim-systematic-flow-shortens-unpurified-circuit-building--367a566abb69\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Procedure as Experimental Infrastructure\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The full rule 110-124 circuit is evaluated through two pairs of dual-rail fluorescence outputs. At 24 hours, the plotted outputs reproduce the intended image relationships: a dog image, its inverse, and corresponding mirror images. This is the central integration evidence because it combines compiler design, unpurified-component preparation, calibration, outlier correction, and tuning in one working circuit.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"claim-full-circuit-recovers-ideal-ca-patterns--367a566abb69\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Full-Circuit Readout\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The synthesis-error-aware model helps explain why the unpurified circuit should be read around 24 hours rather than the roughly 8-hour timing suggested by a purified-circuit model. The paper\\u2019s own limitations are clear: sequence-dependent defects, stoichiometry errors, and leak reactions remain obstacles. The result is best interpreted as a validated design-and-debug methodology, not proof that all future DNA circuits will compile and run without expert intervention.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"claim-synthesis-error-model-explains-delayed-outputs--367a566abb69\",\n              \"claim-signal-restoration-compensates-for-impurity--367a566abb69\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Modeling Assumptions and Limits\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"summary_long\": \"The source presents a practical bridge between molecular compiler design and bench-top DNA strand displacement construction. Its central contribution is not just that the Seesaw Compiler can translate digital logic into seesaw DNA circuits, but that the resulting circuit can be built using unpurified components under a systematic experimental procedure. The paper targets a combined rule 110 and rule 124 cellular-automata logic function, which gives the demonstration a recognizable computational structure while avoiding a spatial cellular automaton implementation; the computation occurs in a well-mixed reaction. The compiled molecular circuit contains six layers and 78 distinct initial DNA species, making it large relative to many earlier strand-displacement demonstrations. The evidence path is staged. First, the compiler produces dual-rail logic, seesaw circuit structure, Visual DSD and Mathematica simulation artifacts, and DNA sequence outputs. Second, the authors calibrate effective concentrations for signal, threshold, and gate species because unpurified strands do not behave like ideal stoichiometric components. The reported threshold values for two- and three-input OR and AND gates show how measured ratios are turned into design choices. Third, the paper confronts composition failures by identifying outlier gates, including one measured gate ratio near 0.44x compared with another near 0.8x, and by tuning downstream behavior. Fourth, the full circuit is evaluated with fluorescence outputs at 24 hours, where the reported dual-rail patterns match the intended cellular automata image relationships. Finally, a model that includes synthesis-error populations semi-quantitatively explains why the unpurified circuit behaves on a slower timescale than a purified-circuit model would predict. The practical interpretation is that molecular compilers need not stop at sequence generation: for unpurified circuits, useful compilation must be paired with calibration protocols, debugging simulations, outlier handling, and models of imperfect molecular populations. The limits are equally important. The paper does not claim arbitrary plug-and-play reliability; it explicitly notes continuing needs to reduce leak reactions and make building blocks less sensitive to synthesis and stoichiometry errors.\",\n        \"summary_short\": \"This Nature Communications article reports a compiler-aided workflow for designing and building a large DNA strand displacement circuit from unpurified oligonucleotides. The demonstrated rule 110-124 circuit contains six layers and 78 initial DNA species, and the authors show how calibration, outlier detection, tuning, and synthesis-error modeling make the construction experimentally workable.\"\n      },\n      \"source_extraction\": {\n        \"converter_name\": \"pdftotext\",\n        \"converter_version\": null,\n        \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n        \"status\": \"success\",\n        \"warnings\": []\n      },\n      \"source_id\": \"source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69\",\n      \"source_kind\": \"file\",\n      \"source_locator\": \"05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits.pdf\",\n      \"source_media_type\": \"application/pdf\",\n      \"source_semantic\": {\n        \"authors\": [\n          \"Anupama J. Thubagere\",\n          \"Chris Thachuk\",\n          \"Joseph Berleant\",\n          \"Robert F. Johnson\",\n          \"Diana A. Ardelean\",\n          \"Kevin M. Cherry\",\n          \"Lulu Qian\"\n        ],\n        \"display_title\": \"Compiler-Aided DNA Strand Displacement Circuits\"\n      },\n      \"source_type\": \"file\",\n      \"summary\": \"Thubagere and coauthors demonstrate a compiler-aided workflow for constructing a six-layer, 78-species DNA strand displacement circuit from unpurified components. The paper combines automated Seesaw Compiler design, effective concentration calibration, outlier correction, output tuning, fluorescence validation, and a synthesis-error-aware model to make large unpurified seesaw circuits more accessible.\",\n      \"title\": \"05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits\",\n      \"warnings\": [\n        \"Task context listed publication date as unresolved, but the source markdown front matter states Published 23 Feb 2017.\",\n        \"The source markdown extraction is marked usable; no PDF fallback was used for layout-sensitive recovery.\",\n        \"Some mathematical notation in extracted text was normalized to ASCII for JSON readability.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"topics\": [\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-effective-concentration-calibration-guides-thresholds--367a566abb69\",\n        \"claim-outlier-gates-expose-synthesis-variation--367a566abb69\",\n        \"claim-systematic-flow-shortens-unpurified-circuit-building--367a566abb69\",\n        \"claim-synthesis-error-model-explains-delayed-outputs--367a566abb69\",\n        \"claim-signal-restoration-compensates-for-impurity--367a566abb69\",\n        \"claim-execution-profiles-integrated-into-an-optimizing-c-compiler-can-guide-classic-op--20be52b7603e\",\n        \"claim-weighted-flow-graphs-expose-hot-paths-by-attaching-execution-counts-to-basic-blo--49c47f8431d5\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compilation-can-make-compiler-optimization-more-evidence--20aeeff3a32c\",\n        \"claim-program-features-provide-the-quantifiable-representation-that-allows-learning-al--a254f7b701be\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compiler-optimization-is-limited-by-the-quality-represent--f9c7d385cc26\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Several sources show compilers using measured or analyzed evidence to steer decisions. The DNA circuit workflow calibrates effective concentrations, identifies outlier gates, tunes output behavior, and uses a synthesis-error-aware model. Profile-guided and machine-learning compiler work similarly use runtime profiles, empirical data, and program features to guide optimization decisions.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The evidence differs by domain: DNA compilation uses biochemical calibration and fluorescence behavior, profile-guided C optimization uses execution counts on weighted flow graphs, and machine-learning compilation uses training data and quantifiable program features. A topic can synthesize these as feedback loops that make compiler decisions depend on observed system behavior rather than fixed heuristics alone.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Forms of evidence\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The sources also warn that evidence quality matters. Machine-learning optimization is limited by training-data representativeness, while unpurified seesaw circuits remain sensitive to synthesis, stoichiometry, and leak-reaction concerns even after calibration.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Limits\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69\",\n        \"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\n        \"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Calibration and feedback in evidence-driven compilation\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-calibration-and-feedback-in-evidence-driven-compilation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-seesaw-compiler-automates-dna-circuit-design--367a566abb69\",\n        \"claim-rule-110-124-circuit-has-78-species--367a566abb69\",\n        \"claim-unpurified-strands-can-build-complex-circuits--367a566abb69\",\n        \"claim-distributed-quantum-computing-requires-compilers-that-can-map-arbitrary-quantum--e4ea92cd41cd\",\n        \"claim-distributed-quantum-computing-can-let-remote-quantum-processing-units-cooperate--c8a669f6488f\",\n        \"claim-distributed-quantum-compilers-must-model-temporal-coupling-map-dynamics-because--0883f72a302d\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The DNA strand displacement and distributed quantum computing sources both treat compilation as a bridge from an abstract computation to a constrained physical substrate. In the DNA case, feedforward digital logic is translated into dual-rail and seesaw DNA circuits with simulation code and DNA sequences. In the distributed quantum case, arbitrary quantum algorithms must be mapped to networked processor architectures while accounting for communication constraints.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The shared page concept should emphasize that these compilers are not only choosing code transformations: they encode substrate-specific limits such as unpurified molecule behavior, effective concentrations, remote Bell-state availability, communication latency, and processor topology.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Physical constraints\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The cross-source connection is conceptual rather than experimental: each source gives strong evidence for compiler-mediated mapping to a specialized substrate, but the substrates and validation methods differ substantially.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Evidence strength\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69\",\n        \"source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Compiler-aided design for nontraditional computing substrates\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-compiler-aided-design-of-nontraditional-computing-substrates\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compilation-can-make-compiler-optimization-more-evidence--20aeeff3a32c\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compilation-can-use-a-model-inside-the-compiler-to-choose--f8131a5c483d\",\n        \"claim-program-features-provide-the-quantifiable-representation-that-allows-learning-al--a254f7b701be\",\n        \"claim-feature-selection-and-dimension-reduction-are-necessary-in-compiler-learning-pro--ecd12a4a4f1f\",\n        \"claim-supervised-learning-for-compiler-optimization-learns-correlations-between-progra--217abf25c992\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compiler-optimization-is-limited-by-the-quality-represent--f9c7d385cc26\",\n        \"claim-execution-profiles-integrated-into-an-optimizing-c-compiler-can-guide-classic-op--20be52b7603e\",\n        \"claim-wcc-couples-a-c-compiler-to-the-ait-static-wcet-analyzer-so-that-wcet-data-is-pr--13f9763eb2a9\",\n        \"claim-wcc-s-back-annotation-raises-assembly-level-wcet-information-to-source-level-icd--fc00d3acd6de\",\n        \"claim-wcc-s-loop-analyzer-computes-safe-loop-iteration-bounds-for-most-benchmark-loops--5eb419e33259\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The sources collectively support a topic about compilers as decision systems that use evidence about programs, executions, hardware, or timing models to select transformations. Machine-learning compilation uses empirical training data and program features to predict optimization choices, profile-guided optimization uses runtime counts to select hot super-block regions, and WCC uses static analyzer output to choose WCET-reducing transformations.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Machine-learning compilation separates model learning from deployment and relies on quantifiable program features, empirical labels, and careful feature selection. Profile-guided classic optimization similarly turns runtime behavior into compiler guidance, using execution profiles to focus conventional transformations on frequently run regions.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Learning and profiling evidence\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"WCC broadens the evidence-driven idea from average-case performance to worst-case timing. It integrates aiT static analysis into compilation, back-annotates assembly-level WCET facts to source-level structures, computes safe loop bounds, and applies optimizations whose value is judged by their impact on the WCEP rather than by profiling input data.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Static timing evidence\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The sources also show important limits. Machine learning depends on feature quality, representative data, and legality analyses supplied by compiler writers; profile-guided optimization depends on useful profiling inputs; WCC depends on safe static analysis and may trade timing improvements against code-size growth. These contrasts make the shared concept stronger rather than collapsing the methods into one technique.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Limits and contrasts\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\n        \"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\n        \"source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Compiler Optimization as Evidence-Driven Decision Making\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-compiler-optimization-as-evidence-driven-decision-making\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-profile-based-classic-optimizations-are-intended-to-complement-conventional-opti--6bcefbcf9607\",\n        \"claim-classic-scalar-optimizations-can-be-reformulated-to-operate-on-super-blocks-and--79ce55378df0\",\n        \"claim-the-implementation-cost-of-profile-based-classic-optimization-was-modest-relativ--062df22ec16c\",\n        \"claim-the-machine-learning-compiler-workflow-separates-model-learning-from-deployment--a04ee1ab9738\",\n        \"claim-direct-prediction-of-the-best-compiler-option-is-practical-for-relatively-small--fe68da808ef3\",\n        \"claim-distributed-quantum-computing-requires-compilers-that-can-map-arbitrary-quantum--e4ea92cd41cd\",\n        \"claim-the-proposed-compiler-is-computationally-efficient-because-distributing-a-circui--b9f318c6234c\",\n        \"claim-wcc-couples-a-c-compiler-to-the-ait-static-wcet-analyzer-so-that-wcet-data-is-pr--13f9763eb2a9\",\n        \"claim-interprocedural-slicing-and-polyhedral-loop-evaluation-substantially-reduce-loop--8f361a6ac310\",\n        \"claim-the-wcc-optimizations-compose-applying-register-allocation-code-and-data-scratch--1393cb5b3616\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Across the sources, compiler improvements are presented as integrated infrastructures rather than isolated transformations. Profile-guided optimization adds profiling, super-block formation, and profile-aware versions of classic optimizations; machine-learning compilation adds training and deployment stages around the compiler; WCC connects a C compiler to aiT and composes multiple WCET-aware optimizations; distributed quantum compilation maps algorithms to constrained distributed architectures through strategy-specific compilation of remote operations.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Profile-guided classic optimization is explicitly complementary to conventional optimization, reformulating classic scalar transformations for super-blocks and super-block loops. Machine-learning compilation similarly inserts a learned model into the compiler after offline training, making optimization decisions for new programs rather than replacing the rest of the compiler pipeline.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Classical and learned compiler pipelines\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"WCC provides a strong infrastructure example because it couples static analysis to compilation, raises timing facts back to source-level structures, accelerates loop analysis through slicing and polyhedral evaluation, and composes register allocation, code scratchpad allocation, data scratchpad allocation, and procedure positioning. The matmult case shows these optimizations can combine into a much larger WCET reduction than an isolated pass would suggest.\",\n          \"heading\": \"WCET-aware composition\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The sources target different objectives: average speed, learned heuristic selection, worst-case execution time, and distributed quantum feasibility or depth. A synthesis page can use this contrast to explain how modern compiler frameworks embed domain-specific evidence and passes while preserving a broader compiler workflow.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Domain-specific objectives\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\n        \"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\n        \"source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d\",\n        \"source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Compositional Compiler Infrastructure for Domain-Specific Objectives\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-compositional-compiler-infrastructure-for-domain-specific-objectives\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-profile-based-classic-optimizations-are-intended-to-complement-conventional-opti--6bcefbcf9607\",\n        \"claim-the-profile-based-transformations-controlled-executable-size-growth-averaging-1--5bacab4b8a28\",\n        \"claim-the-experiments-used-separate-profiling-inputs-and-performance-measurement-input--ee5ac2fa5a94\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compilation-can-use-a-model-inside-the-compiler-to-choose--f8131a5c483d\",\n        \"claim-thread-coarsening-decisions-are-context-dependent-because-coarsening-can-improve--4878860bda89\",\n        \"claim-direct-prediction-of-the-best-compiler-option-is-practical-for-relatively-small--fe68da808ef3\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Both sources emphasize that optimization choices are context dependent rather than universally beneficial. Profile-based classic optimizations target hot paths and complement conventional optimization, while machine-learning examples such as thread coarsening depend on program and hardware context.\",\n          \"heading\": \"No Universally Best Optimization\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The profile-guided work manages tradeoffs such as executable-size growth while seeking speedups. The machine-learning work highlights tradeoffs among parallelism, register pressure, memory behavior, instruction-level parallelism, code size, speed, and power.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Competing Objectives\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"This topic can frame compiler optimization as selecting transformations under competing objectives, with profiling inputs, benchmark measurements, and learned predictions used to identify when a transformation is likely to pay off.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Evidence for Payoff Decisions\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\n        \"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Context-Dependent Optimization Tradeoffs\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-context-dependent-optimization-tradeoffs--d53df7de5590\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-execution-profiles-integrated-into-an-optimizing-c-compiler-can-guide-classic-op--20be52b7603e\",\n        \"claim-weighted-flow-graphs-expose-hot-paths-by-attaching-execution-counts-to-basic-blo--49c47f8431d5\",\n        \"claim-on-the-benchmark-suite-profile-based-super-block-optimizations-averaged-a-1-15-s--e5d7486c0e56\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compilation-can-use-a-model-inside-the-compiler-to-choose--f8131a5c483d\",\n        \"claim-the-machine-learning-compiler-workflow-separates-model-learning-from-deployment--a04ee1ab9738\",\n        \"claim-supervised-learning-for-compiler-optimization-learns-correlations-between-progra--217abf25c992\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compilation-can-make-compiler-optimization-more-evidence--20aeeff3a32c\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compiler-optimization-is-limited-by-the-quality-represent--f9c7d385cc26\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Both sources treat compiler optimization as an evidence-guided decision problem. The profile-guided source uses execution counts and weighted flow graphs to focus classic C optimizations on frequently executed regions, while the machine-learning source uses empirical training data and learned models to choose optimization heuristics for unseen programs.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared Evidence Model\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The profile-guided work grounds decisions in runtime counts and reports measured speedups for super-block optimizations. The machine-learning work generalizes empirical evidence into models that predict optimization choices from program features and separates learning from deployment inside the compiler.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Profiles and Learned Models\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"A synthesis page should compare profile counts, supervised labels, feature values, and performance measurements as related optimization evidence while noting that learned compiler models depend on the quality, cost, and representativeness of their training data.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Limits of Evidence\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\n        \"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Empirical Evidence as a Compiler Optimization Signal\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-empirical-evidence-as-a-compiler-optimization-signal--7baebd1fca65\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-execution-profiles-integrated-into-an-optimizing-c-compiler-can-guide-classic-op--20be52b7603e\",\n        \"claim-weighted-flow-graphs-expose-hot-paths-by-attaching-execution-counts-to-basic-blo--49c47f8431d5\",\n        \"claim-classic-scalar-optimizations-can-be-reformulated-to-operate-on-super-blocks-and--79ce55378df0\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compilation-can-use-a-model-inside-the-compiler-to-choose--f8131a5c483d\",\n        \"claim-program-features-provide-the-quantifiable-representation-that-allows-learning-al--a254f7b701be\",\n        \"claim-supervised-learning-for-compiler-optimization-learns-correlations-between-progra--217abf25c992\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compilation-can-make-compiler-optimization-more-evidence--20aeeff3a32c\",\n        \"claim-distributed-quantum-computing-can-let-remote-quantum-processing-units-cooperate--c8a669f6488f\",\n        \"claim-distributed-quantum-computing-requires-compilers-that-can-map-arbitrary-quantum--e4ea92cd41cd\",\n        \"claim-remote-cnot-execution-consumes-bell-state-resources-and-is-limited-by-the-number--ff11e4d2be8e\",\n        \"claim-distributed-quantum-compilers-must-model-temporal-coupling-map-dynamics-because--0883f72a302d\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The sources present compiler optimization as a decision process improved by evidence about program behavior, program representation, hardware context, or execution constraints rather than by fixed local rules alone. Profile-guided C optimization uses run-time execution information to focus classic transformations on hot regions. Machine-learning compilation trains empirical models from program features and performance labels. Distributed quantum compilation must account for network timing, Bell-state availability, communication latency, and communication-qubit limits when mapping algorithms to distributed hardware.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Weighted flow graphs and execution profiles expose frequently executed classical paths; feature vectors make source, intermediate-representation, and dynamic properties learnable; distributed quantum coupling maps encode remote-operation constraints that change over time. These evidence sources differ in form, but each gives the compiler a basis for deciding where transformations or mappings are likely to pay off.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Profile, learned, and architectural evidence\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"A synthesis page can compare profiling counts, learned program features, and distributed coupling-map dynamics as parallel answers to the same compiler-design question: what evidence should guide optimization decisions when costs depend on context?\",\n          \"heading\": \"Synthesis value\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\n        \"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\n        \"source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Evidence-guided compilation for context-dependent optimization\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-evidence-guided-compilation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-weighted-flow-graphs-expose-hot-paths-by-attaching-execution-counts-to-basic-blo--49c47f8431d5\",\n        \"claim-super-blocks-represent-frequently-executed-paths-as-linear-basic-block-sequences--7bbb510c9bb0\",\n        \"claim-trace-selection-followed-by-tail-duplication-converts-frequently-executed-traces--1483ee9d0534\",\n        \"claim-the-major-effectiveness-mechanisms-are-eliminating-entries-into-the-middle-of-tr--4bc0ff5e21b0\",\n        \"claim-on-the-benchmark-suite-profile-based-super-block-optimizations-averaged-a-1-15-s--e5d7486c0e56\",\n        \"claim-static-wcet-reduction-depends-on-optimizing-the-worst-case-execution-path-becaus--b6a70fae6e3d\",\n        \"claim-wcc-s-back-annotation-raises-assembly-level-wcet-information-to-source-level-icd--fc00d3acd6de\",\n        \"claim-wcet-aware-register-allocation-lowers-wcet-by-avoiding-spill-code-on-the-wcep-ev--a21ac879b656\",\n        \"claim-the-wcc-optimizations-compose-applying-register-allocation-code-and-data-scratch--1393cb5b3616\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Several compiler strategies become more effective when they focus optimization effort on the execution paths that matter most. Profile-guided classic optimization identifies frequently executed paths using weighted flow graphs and converts them into super-blocks, while WCC uses static WCET analysis to identify the worst-case execution path and direct transformations toward timing-critical regions. The common concept is path-sensitive feedback: measured hotness and statically derived worst-case criticality are different evidence sources for deciding where optimizations should be concentrated.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The profile-guided source describes weighted flow graphs with execution counts on basic blocks and taken counts on control-flow arcs, then uses trace selection and tail duplication to form super-blocks from frequently executed traces. Its effectiveness claims emphasize eliminating entries into the middle of traces and optimizing the most frequently executed loop path, with benchmark speedups and controlled code growth.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Evidence from profile-guided compilation\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The WCC source frames timing improvement around the worst-case execution path: reducing a noncritical block may leave global WCET unchanged, so optimizations use WCEP membership, execution frequencies, cache misses, spill-code data, and other back-annotated facts. Register allocation, scratchpad allocation, and composed optimization sequences then reduce WCET by prioritizing the statically critical path.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Evidence from WCET-aware compilation\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"This topic can compare dynamic hot-path optimization with static worst-case-path optimization without treating them as the same problem. Both sources support a broader page about compiler feedback loops that select regions, reshape control flow or placement, and trade local costs such as code size or spill code against path-level performance goals.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Why this supports a topic page\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\n        \"source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Feedback-Directed Optimization of Critical Execution Paths\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-feedback-directed-critical-path-optimization\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-tail-duplication-creates-additional-optimization-opportunities-because-it-can-ma--6c81300e0307\",\n        \"claim-the-major-effectiveness-mechanisms-are-eliminating-entries-into-the-middle-of-tr--4bc0ff5e21b0\",\n        \"claim-the-implementation-cost-of-profile-based-classic-optimization-was-modest-relativ--062df22ec16c\",\n        \"claim-supervised-learning-for-compiler-optimization-learns-correlations-between-progra--217abf25c992\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compiler-optimization-is-limited-by-the-quality-represent--f9c7d385cc26\",\n        \"claim-future-research-in-machine-learning-compilation-extends-beyond-choosing-optimiza--faf587f1fc17\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The profile-guided source provides concrete mechanisms for exposing and optimizing hot paths: trace selection, tail duplication, eliminating entries into the middle of traces, and focusing optimization on frequently executed loop paths.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Mechanisms for Exposing Hot Paths\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The machine-learning source describes a broader policy-learning view in which models predict optimization decisions from program features, but it also states that learning cannot invent legal transformations or derive legality analyses by itself.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Policy Learning Still Needs Compiler Machinery\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"A synthesis page could connect hand-engineered hot-path transformations with learned policies: profiles and features can guide when to apply transformations, while compiler-provided legality checks and transformation machinery remain necessary.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Connecting Mechanism and Policy\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\n        \"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"From Hot-Path Mechanisms to Learned Optimization Policies\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-from-hot-path-mechanisms-to-learned-optimization-policies--5d71ef3606c7\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-super-blocks-represent-frequently-executed-paths-as-linear-basic-block-sequences--7bbb510c9bb0\",\n        \"claim-trace-selection-followed-by-tail-duplication-converts-frequently-executed-traces--1483ee9d0534\",\n        \"claim-classic-scalar-optimizations-can-be-reformulated-to-operate-on-super-blocks-and--79ce55378df0\",\n        \"claim-program-features-provide-the-quantifiable-representation-that-allows-learning-al--a254f7b701be\",\n        \"claim-feature-selection-and-dimension-reduction-are-necessary-in-compiler-learning-pro--ecd12a4a4f1f\",\n        \"claim-future-research-in-machine-learning-compilation-extends-beyond-choosing-optimiza--faf587f1fc17\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The sources share a concern with how programs are represented before optimization decisions are made. The profile-guided source reshapes hot control-flow paths into super-blocks and super-block loops, while the machine-learning source represents programs through quantifiable static, dynamic, or combined features.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Representation Before Optimization\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Super-block formation makes frequently executed paths easier for classic scalar optimizations to exploit. Feature engineering, feature selection, and dimension reduction make program behavior and structure available to learning algorithms without overwhelming models with redundant or sparse feature spaces.\",\n          \"heading\": \"What Optimizers Can See\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The topic should preserve the distinction between structural compiler regions and statistical feature vectors, but can synthesize them as representation layers that determine what an optimizer can observe, compare, and act upon.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Structural and Statistical Views\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\n        \"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Program Representation Choices Shape Optimization Opportunities\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-program-representation-choices-shape-optimization-opportunities--d64189e12b24\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-super-blocks-represent-frequently-executed-paths-as-linear-basic-block-sequences--7bbb510c9bb0\",\n        \"claim-trace-selection-followed-by-tail-duplication-converts-frequently-executed-traces--1483ee9d0534\",\n        \"claim-tail-duplication-creates-additional-optimization-opportunities-because-it-can-ma--6c81300e0307\",\n        \"claim-feature-selection-and-dimension-reduction-are-necessary-in-compiler-learning-pro--ecd12a4a4f1f\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compiler-optimization-is-limited-by-the-quality-represent--f9c7d385cc26\",\n        \"claim-future-research-in-machine-learning-compilation-extends-beyond-choosing-optimiza--faf587f1fc17\",\n        \"claim-distributed-quantum-computing-can-let-remote-quantum-processing-units-cooperate--c8a669f6488f\",\n        \"claim-the-paper-s-worst-case-system-model-uses-one-data-qubit-per-processor-and-a-one--9e5973bc3485\",\n        \"claim-entanglement-swapping-augments-distributed-processor-connectivity-by-enabling-re--b0cac39f5fb5\",\n        \"claim-the-data-qubit-swapping-based-strategy-arranges-data-qubits-so-each-cnot-in-a-la--ba4ebf62d172\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Across the sources, the representation chosen by the compiler determines which optimization opportunities, dependencies, and constraints become visible. Profile-guided classic optimization turns hot traces into super-blocks. Machine-learning optimization depends on feature representations and dimension reduction. Distributed quantum compilation represents a networked quantum machine through processors, communication qubits, quantum links, topology constraints, and remote operations.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Super-blocks are linear basic-block sequences with a single entry and multiple exits, formed by trace selection and tail duplication to expose hot-path transformations. Compiler-learning systems use quantifiable program features, but high-dimensional feature spaces often require pruning or projection before models can generalize. Distributed quantum compilers model worst-case and topology-dependent connectivity so remote CNOTs, entanglement swapping, and data-qubit placement can be reasoned about explicitly.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Representation choices\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"A synthesis page can treat super-blocks, feature vectors, and quantum coupling maps as examples of compiler representations that are not neutral: each representation decides what the optimizer can see, what costs it can estimate, and what transformations or mappings it can legally consider.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Synthesis value\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\n        \"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\n        \"source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Representations that define optimization scope\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-representations-for-optimization-scope\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-ilp-based-code-scratchpad-allocation-lowers-wcet-by-moving-beneficial-basic-bloc--d5d88da4e90e\",\n        \"claim-ilp-based-data-scratchpad-allocation-lowers-wcet-by-moving-frequently-accessed-v--8ceca90523fc\",\n        \"claim-wcet-centric-procedure-positioning-reduces-instruction-cache-conflict-misses-by--47eeb6643a90\",\n        \"claim-wcet-aware-procedure-cloning-improves-wcet-estimates-by-specializing-functions-w--808a9c5da846\",\n        \"claim-remote-cnot-execution-consumes-bell-state-resources-and-is-limited-by-the-number--ff11e4d2be8e\",\n        \"claim-distributed-quantum-compilers-must-model-temporal-coupling-map-dynamics-because--0883f72a302d\",\n        \"claim-entanglement-swapping-augments-distributed-processor-connectivity-by-enabling-re--b0cac39f5fb5\",\n        \"claim-the-data-qubit-swapping-based-strategy-arranges-data-qubits-so-each-cnot-in-a-la--ba4ebf62d172\",\n        \"claim-experimental-validation-on-medium-size-quantum-circuits-respected-the-analytical--82f1bfd54f5c\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Compiler optimization in the provided sources is often constrained by scarce architectural resources. WCC allocates code and data to limited scratchpad memories and reorders or clones procedures to affect cache behavior; distributed quantum compilation schedules remote operations under limits from Bell-state availability, communication qubits, topology, and regeneration latency. Both cases require the compiler to model resource capacity and communication or memory costs rather than only local instruction transformations.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The WCC source supports resource-aware compilation through ILP-based code and data scratchpad allocation, WCET-centric procedure positioning, and WCET-aware procedure cloning. These transformations reduce worst-case timing by placing beneficial regions or variables in faster memories, reducing instruction-cache conflict misses, or specializing calls, while also exposing tradeoffs such as code-size growth.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Classical embedded timing resources\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The distributed quantum source supports the same high-level theme in a different architecture. Remote CNOT execution consumes Bell states, is limited by communication qubits, and requires coupling maps that model temporal dynamics. Entanglement swapping can create virtual links but consumes intermediate Bell states, while data-qubit swapping changes placement so CNOTs operate on neighboring processors.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Distributed quantum resources\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The evidence justifies a concept-centric topic about resource-aware compiler mapping, not a claim that classical WCET compilers and quantum compilers use identical algorithms. The shared issue is that compilation quality depends on representing limited hardware resources and choosing transformations that reduce the dominant cost under those constraints.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Synthesis boundary\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d\",\n        \"source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Resource-Aware Compilation Under Architectural Constraints\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-resource-aware-compilation-under-architectural-constraints\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-full-circuit-recovers-ideal-ca-patterns--367a566abb69\",\n        \"claim-signal-restoration-compensates-for-impurity--367a566abb69\",\n        \"claim-effective-concentration-calibration-guides-thresholds--367a566abb69\",\n        \"claim-static-wcet-reduction-depends-on-optimizing-the-worst-case-execution-path-becaus--b6a70fae6e3d\",\n        \"claim-ilp-based-code-scratchpad-allocation-lowers-wcet-by-moving-beneficial-basic-bloc--d5d88da4e90e\",\n        \"claim-wcet-aware-register-allocation-lowers-wcet-by-avoiding-spill-code-on-the-wcep-ev--a21ac879b656\",\n        \"claim-remote-cnot-execution-consumes-bell-state-resources-and-is-limited-by-the-number--ff11e4d2be8e\",\n        \"claim-the-data-qubit-swapping-based-strategy-arranges-data-qubits-so-each-cnot-in-a-la--ba4ebf62d172\",\n        \"claim-experimental-validation-on-medium-size-quantum-circuits-respected-the-analytical--82f1bfd54f5c\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The DNA, WCET, and distributed quantum sources all frame compilation as resource-aware planning under hard constraints. DNA circuit construction must cope with molecular impurities, thresholds, gate variation, and leak sensitivity. WCC optimizes worst-case execution paths and scratchpad, register, cache, and procedure-placement resources. Distributed quantum compilation schedules remote operations constrained by Bell states, communication qubits, and link regeneration.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The optimization goals are domain-specific: restoring intended logic behavior in DNA circuits, reducing worst-case execution time in embedded C programs, and lowering communication-depth overhead in distributed quantum circuits. The shared abstraction is that compiler transformations become valuable when they are guided by the scarce resource that controls end-to-end success.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Optimization target\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The evidence supports the topic through measured or analyzed outcomes, including the full DNA circuit matching intended cellular automata pattern relationships, WCC reductions from scratchpad and register-allocation strategies, and quantum experiments respecting analytical bounds.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Validation\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69\",\n        \"source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e\",\n        \"source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Resource-aware compilation under physical and timing constraints\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-resource-aware-compilation-under-physical-and-timing-constraints\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-profile-based-classic-optimizations-are-intended-to-complement-conventional-opti--6bcefbcf9607\",\n        \"claim-on-the-benchmark-suite-profile-based-super-block-optimizations-averaged-a-1-15-s--e5d7486c0e56\",\n        \"claim-the-profile-based-transformations-controlled-executable-size-growth-averaging-1--5bacab4b8a28\",\n        \"claim-the-experiments-used-separate-profiling-inputs-and-performance-measurement-input--ee5ac2fa5a94\",\n        \"claim-the-implementation-cost-of-profile-based-classic-optimization-was-modest-relativ--062df22ec16c\",\n        \"claim-direct-prediction-of-the-best-compiler-option-is-practical-for-relatively-small--fe68da808ef3\",\n        \"claim-thread-coarsening-decisions-are-context-dependent-because-coarsening-can-improve--4878860bda89\",\n        \"claim-machine-learning-based-compiler-optimization-is-limited-by-the-quality-represent--f9c7d385cc26\",\n        \"claim-the-major-effectiveness-mechanisms-are-eliminating-entries-into-the-middle-of-tr--4bc0ff5e21b0\",\n        \"claim-remote-cnot-execution-consumes-bell-state-resources-and-is-limited-by-the-number--ff11e4d2be8e\",\n        \"claim-in-the-worst-case-topology-the-entanglement-swapping-based-strategy-has-per-laye--bd58d42b2d90\",\n        \"claim-the-data-qubit-swapping-based-strategy-arranges-data-qubits-so-each-cnot-in-a-la--ba4ebf62d172\",\n        \"claim-the-proposed-compiler-is-computationally-efficient-because-distributing-a-circui--b9f318c6234c\",\n        \"claim-experimental-validation-on-medium-size-quantum-circuits-respected-the-analytical--82f1bfd54f5c\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The sources frame compiler optimization as a tradeoff problem whose success must be measured rather than assumed. Profile-based classic optimizations report speedup while tracking executable-size growth and using separate profiling and measurement inputs. Machine-learning compilation learns from empirical performance data but remains constrained by training quality, representativeness, and context-dependent optimization effects. Distributed quantum compilation compares remote-operation strategies under topology, depth, Bell-pair consumption, and communication-resource constraints.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Profile-based super-block optimizations are described as complements to conventional optimization, with measured speedups and controlled code growth. Machine-learning approaches can directly predict compiler choices for bounded option spaces, while examples such as thread coarsening show that a choice can improve some performance factors and harm others. Distributed quantum strategies such as entanglement swapping and data-qubit swapping are evaluated through analytical depth bounds, computational complexity, and medium-size circuit experiments.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Measured costs and benefits\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"A synthesis page can organize compiler strategies by the costs they trade off: runtime versus code size in profile-guided optimization, training-data cost versus prediction quality in machine-learning compilation, and communication depth versus Bell-state or qubit-resource consumption in distributed quantum compilation.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Synthesis value\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\n        \"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\n        \"source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Tradeoff-aware compiler strategies and empirical validation\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-tradeoff-aware-compiler-strategies\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": []\n}\n\n\nsources records:\nverification/example_site/spaces/compilers/sources/records/source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf.json\nverification/example_site/spaces/compilers/sources/records/source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d.json\nverification/example_site/spaces/compilers/sources/records/source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d.json\nverification/example_site/spaces/compilers/sources/records/source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e.json\nverification/example_site/spaces/compilers/sources/records/source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69.json\n\nclaims:\nverification/example_site/spaces/compilers/claims/claim-classic-scalar-optimizations-can-be-reformulated-to-operate-on-super-blocks-and--79ce55378df0.json\nverification/example_site/spaces/compilers/claims/claim-direct-prediction-of-the-best-compiler-option-is-practical-for-relatively-small--fe68da808ef3.json\nverification/example_site/spaces/compilers/claims/claim-distributed-quantu